 ± 0.032 and an inbreeding coefficient (F<sub>IS</sub>) of 0.1517 ± 0.076, indicating moderate diversity and a relatively low level of inbreeding for an endangered native breed. ADMIXTURE analyses revealed a predominant and distinctive ancestry component exclusive to YBHP, along with shared ancestry with European breeds, particularly those of presumed Iberian origin. Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and phylogenetic network clustering supported its genomic singularity while confirming ancestral proximity to traditional European pigs. These findings highlight the dual nature of the YBHP: a genetically distinct native breed shaped by local adaptation yet retaining ancestral signals from Old World lineages. This genomic insight provides a scientific basis for conservation planning, supports the breed's recognition as a unique genetic resource, and promotes its sustainable use in regional production systems.","affiliation":"Posgrado en Ciencias Biológico Agropecuarias, Universidad Autónoma de Nayarit, Tepic 63315, Mexico.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Asociación Mexicana de Criadores de Cerdos de Origen Iberico de Yucatán and the Secretariat of Research, Innovation, and Higher Education. Mérida, Yucatán, Mexico.","grantId":"50","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Genetic diversity","Livestock","Single-nucleotide polymorphism","Indigenous Breeds","Porcine-Ggp-50k","Yucatán Black Hairless Pig"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"doi","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/vetsci12080755"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/vetsci12080755"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12390364"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12390364?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":3,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2025-09-15","dateOfCreation":"2025-08-28","dateOfRevision":"2025-09-15","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-08-13","firstPublicationDate":"2025-08-13","firstIndexDate":"2025-09-04","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-11-29","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"35122337","source":"MED","pmid":"35122337","pmcid":"PMC9984198","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC9984198"]},"doi":"10.1002/nbm.4698","title":"Prospects and limitations of paramagnetic chemical exchange saturation transfer agents serving as biological reporters in vivo.","authorString":"Sherry AD, Castelli DD, Aime S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sherry AD","lastName":"Sherry","firstName":"A Dean","initials":"AD","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7150-8301"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry, University of Texas at Dallas, Richardson, Texas, USA."},{"affiliation":"Advanced Imaging Research Center, University of Texas Southwestern Medical Center, Dallas, Texas, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Castelli DD","lastName":"Castelli","firstName":"Daniela Delli","initials":"DD","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1806-3141"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Biotechnology and Health Sciences, University of Turin, Turin, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Aime S","lastName":"Aime","firstName":"Silvio","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5373-4708"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Biotechnology and Health Sciences, University of Turin, Turin, Italy."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7150-8301"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5373-4708"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1806-3141"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"36","journalIssueId":3573293,"dateOfPublication":"2023 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2023,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"NMR Biomed","title":"NMR in biomedicine","essn":"1099-1492","issn":"0952-3480","isoabbreviation":"NMR Biomed","nlmid":"8915233"},"printPublicationDate":"2023-06-01"},"pubYear":"2023","pageInfo":"e4698","abstractText":"The concept of using paramagnetic metal ion complexes as chemical exchange saturation transfer agents (paraCEST) for molecular imaging of various biological processes first appeared in the literature about 20 years ago. The first paraCEST agent was based on a highly shifted, inner-sphere, slowly exchanging water molecule that could be activated at a frequency far away from bulk water, a substantial advantage for selective activation of the agent alone. Many other paraCEST agent designs followed that were based on activation of exchanging -NH or -OH proton on the chelate itself. Both types of paraCEST designs are attractive for molecular imaging because the rates of water molecule or ligand proton exchange can be designed to be sensitive to a biological or physiological property such as pH, enzyme activity, or redox. Hence, the intensity or frequency of the resulting CEST signal provides a direct readout of that property. Many molecular designs have appeared in the literature over the past 20 years, mostly reported as proof-of-concept designs but, unfortunately, only a few reports have explored the limitations of paraCEST agents for imaging a biological process in vivo. As a community, we now know that the sensitivity of paraCEST agents is lower than one might anticipate based upon simple chemical exchange principles and, in general, it appears the sensitivity of paraCEST agents is even lower in vivo than in vitro. In this short review, we address some of the factors that contribute to the limited sensitivity of paraCEST agents in vivo, offer some thoughts on approaches that could lead to dramatically improved paraCEST sensitivity, and challenge the scientific community to perform more in vivo experiments designed to test these ideas.","affiliation":"Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry, University of Texas at Dallas, Richardson, Texas, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Review","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","grantId":"R01","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","grantId":"CA115531","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"R01 CA115531","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Protons"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Water"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Contrast Media","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Magnetic Resonance Imaging","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Coordination Complexes"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Molecular Imaging"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Molecular Imaging","Chemical Exchange","Paracest","Responsive Mr Agents"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Protons","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Contrast Media","registryNumber":"0"},{"na