dong Province and Hong Kong Region on Marine Bioresource Conservation and Exploitation, College of Marine Sciences, South China Agricultural University, Guangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chu W","lastName":"Chu","firstName":"Wei","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hung Hom, Kowloon, Hong Kong."}]}},{"fullName":"Huang Y","lastName":"Huang","firstName":"Yumei","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Joint Laboratory of Guangdong Province and Hong Kong Region on Marine Bioresource Conservation and Exploitation, College of Marine Sciences, South China Agricultural University, Guangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu L","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Lijie","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Biology and the Environment, Nanjing Forestry University, Nanjing, 210037, Jiangsu, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen M","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Meijuan","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Human Settlements and Civil Engineering, Xi'an Jiaotong University, Xi'an, Shan Xi, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yan M","lastName":"Yan","firstName":"Muting","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Joint Laboratory of Guangdong Province and Hong Kong Region on Marine Bioresource Conservation and Exploitation, College of Marine Sciences, South China Agricultural University, Guangzhou, China. Electronic address: marineymt@scau.edu.cn."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"276","journalIssueId":3144068,"dateOfPublication":"2021 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2021,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Environ Pollut","title":"Environmental pollution (Barking, Essex : 1987)","essn":"1873-6424","issn":"0269-7491","isoabbreviation":"Environ Pollut","nlmid":"8804476"},"printPublicationDate":"2021-05-01"},"pubYear":"2021","pageInfo":"116691","abstractText":"The environmental-friendly photocatalytic process with a magnetic catalyst CoFe<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>/TiO<sub>2</sub> mediated by solar light for ibuprofen (IBP) degradation in pure water, wastewater effluent and artificial seawater was investigated systematically. The study aims to reveal the efficiency, the mechanism and toxicity evolution during IBP degradation. Hydroxyl radicals and photo-hole (h<sup>+</sup>) were found to contribute to the IBP decay. The presence of SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2-</sup> showed no significant effect, while NO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> accelerated the photodegradation, and other anions including HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>, Cl<sup>-</sup>, F<sup>-</sup>, and Br<sup>-</sup> showed significant inhibition. The removal efficiency was significantly elevated with the addition of peroxymonosulfate (PMS) or persulfate (PS) ([Oxidant]<sub>0</sub>:[IBP]<sub>0</sub> = 0.4-4), with reaction rate of 5.3-13.1 and 1.3-2.9 times as high as the control group, respectively. However, the reaction was slowed down with the introduction of H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>. A mathematic model was employed to describe the effect of ferrate, high concentration or stepwise addition of ferrate was suggested to play a positive role in IBP photodegradation. Thirteen transformation products were identified and five of them were newly reported. The degradation pathways including hydroxylation, the benzene ring opening and the oxidation of carbon were proposed. IBP can be efficiently removed when spiked in wastewater and seawater despite the decreased degradation rate by 41% and 56%, respectively. Compared to the IBP removal, mineralization was relatively lower. The adverse effect of the parent compound IBP to the green algae Chlorella vulgaris was gradually eliminated with the decomposition of IBP. The transformation product C178a which possibly posed toxicity to rotifers Brachionus calyciflorus can also be efficiently removed, indicating that the photocatalysis process is effective in IBP removal, mineralization and toxicity elimination.","affiliation":"Joint Laboratory of Guangdong Province and Hong Kong Region on Marine Bioresource Conservation and Exploitation, College of Marine Sciences, South China Agricultural University, Guangzhou, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Hong Kong Polytechnic University","grantId":"G-YBHP","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Guangzhou Municipal Science and Technology Project","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grantId":"41807476","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Chlorella vulgaris"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Hydrogen Peroxide"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Ibuprofen","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TO","qualifierName":"toxicity"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Water Pollutants, Chemical","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AN","qualifierName":"analysis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TO","qualifierName":"toxicity"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Catalysis"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Degradation","Pharmaceutical","Photocatalyst","Solar Light"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Water Pollutants, Chemical","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Hydrogen Peroxide","registryNumber":"BBX060AN9V"},{"name":"Ibuprofen","registryNumber":"WK2XYI10QM"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envpol.2021.116691"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N"