uations":"N"},{"id":"40430805","source":"MED","pmid":"40430805","pmcid":"PMC12114223","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12114223"]},"doi":"10.3390/pathogens14050485","title":"From the Gut to the Brain: Transcriptomic Insights into Neonatal Meningitis &lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt; Across Diverse Host Niches.","authorString":"Edison LK, Kariyawasam S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Edison LK","lastName":"Edison","firstName":"Lekshmi K","initials":"LK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2599-7428"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Comparative Diagnostics and Population Medicine, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL 32608, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Kariyawasam S","lastName":"Kariyawasam","firstName":"Subhashinie","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2887-7982"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Comparative Diagnostics and Population Medicine, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL 32608, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2599-7428"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2887-7982"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"14","journalIssueId":3948204,"dateOfPublication":"2025 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2025,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Pathogens","title":"Pathogens (Basel, Switzerland)","essn":"2076-0817","issn":"2076-0817","isoabbreviation":"Pathogens","nlmid":"101596317"},"printPublicationDate":"2025-05-01"},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"485","abstractText":"Neonatal Meningitis-causing <i>Escherichia coli</i> (NMEC) is the leading cause of neonatal meningitis and exhibits remarkable adaptability to diverse host environments. Understanding its transcriptional responses across different host niches is crucial for deciphering pathogenesis and identifying potential therapeutic targets. We performed a comparative transcriptomic analysis of NMEC RS218, the prototype strain of NMEC, under four distinct host-mimicking conditions: colonic fluid (CF), serum (S), human brain endothelial cells (HBECs), and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). Differential gene expression analysis was conducted to assess metabolic shifts, virulence factor regulation, and niche-specific adaptation strategies, in which RS218 demonstrated niche-specific transcriptional reprogramming. In CF, genes associated with biofilm formation, motility, efflux pumps, and cell division regulation were upregulated, aiding gut colonization. The serum environment triggered the expression of siderophore-mediated iron acquisition, enterobactin biosynthesis, and heme utilization genes, facilitating immune evasion and bacterial persistence. In HBECs, NMEC upregulated genes linked to nucleoside metabolism, membrane remodeling, pilus organization, and blood-brain barrier (BBB) traversal. In CSF, genes related to oxidative stress resistance, chemotaxis, DNA repair, biofilm formation, and amino acid biosynthesis were enriched, reflecting NMEC's adaptive mechanisms for survival under nutrient-depleted conditions. Energy-intensive pathways were consistently downregulated across all niches, highlighting the need for an energy conservation strategy. This study provides novel insights into NMEC's adaptive strategies across different host environments, emphasizing its metabolic flexibility, virulence regulation, and immune evasion mechanisms, offering potential targets for therapeutic intervention.","affiliation":"Department of Comparative Diagnostics and Population Medicine, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL 32608, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Blood-Brain Barrier","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Brain","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Endothelial Cells","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Escherichia coli","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PY","qualifierName":"pathogenicity"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Meningitis, Escherichia coli","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Virulence Factors","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Expression Profiling"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Expression Regulation, Bacterial"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Infant, Newborn"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Host-Pathogen Interactions"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Transcriptome"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Adherence","Transcriptomics","Host Adaptation","Blood–brain Barrier","Biofilm Formation","Metabolic Reprogramming","Neonatal Meningitis Escherichia Coli (Nmec)"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Virulence Factors","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/pathogens14050485"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12114223"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12114223?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"PPR","id":"PPR1005820","type":"Preprint in","note":"CrossRef Pre-print loader","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":5,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"Y","dbCrossReferenceList":{"dbName":["EMBL"]},"hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["geo"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2025-05-28","dateOfCreation":"2025-05-28","dateOfRevision":"2025-05-31","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-05-15","firstPublicationDate":"2025-05-15","firstIndexDate":"2025-05-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-05-31","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"36939343","source":"MED","pmid":"36939343","pmcid":"PMC10100675","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC10100675"]},"doi":"10.1128/spectrum.04158-22","title":"Sodium Fluoride Exposure Leads to ATP Depletion and Altered RNA Decay in &lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt; under Anaerobic Conditions.","authorString":"Murashko ON, Yeh KH, Yu CA, Kaberdin VR, Lin-Chao S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Murashko ON","lastName":"Murashko","firstName":"Oleg N","initials":"ON","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0389-4284"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Molecular Biology, Academia Sinica, Taipei, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Yeh KH","lastName":"Yeh","firstName":"Kun-Hai","initials":"KH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Molecular Biology, Academia Sinica, Taipei, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Yu CA","lastName":"Yu","firstName":"Chen-Hsin Albert","initials":"CA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5662-3026"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Molecular Biology, Academia Sinica, Taipei, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kaberdin VR","lastName":"Kaberdin","firstName":"Vladimir R","initials":"VR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7166-5136"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Molecular Biology, Academia Sinica, Taipei, Taiwan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Immunology, Microbiology and Parasitology, University of the Basque Country UPV/EHU, Leioa, Spain."},{"affiliation":"Basque Foundation for Science, IKERBASQUE, Bilbao, Spain."},{"affiliation":"Research Centre for Experimental Marine Biology and Biotechnology (PIE-UPV/EHU), Plentzia, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Lin-Chao S","lastName":"Lin-Chao","firstName":"Sue","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8415-9304"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Molecular Biology, Academia Sinica, Taipei, Taiwan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5662-3026"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7166-5136"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8415-9304"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0389-4284"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"11","journalIssueId":3544266,"dateOfPublication":"2023 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2023,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Microbiol Spectr","title":"Microbiology spectrum","essn":"2165-0497","issn":"2165-0497","isoabbreviation":"Microbiol Spectr","nlmid":"101634614"},"printPublicationDate":"2023-04-01"},"pubYear":"2023","pageInfo":"e0415822","abstractText":"Although fluoride-containing compounds are widely used to inhibit bacterial growth, the reprogramming of gene expression underlying cellular responses to fluoride, especially under anaerobic conditions, is still poorly understood. Here, we compare the genome-wide transcriptomic profiles of <i>E. coli</i> grown in the absence (control) or presence (20 and 70 mM) of sodium fluoride (NaF) under anaerobic conditions and assess the impact of fluoride-dependent ATP depletion on RNA turnover. Tiling array analysis revealed transcripts displaying altered abundance in response to NaF treatments. Quantile-based K-means clustering uncovered a subset of genes that were highly upregulated and then downregulated in response to increased and subsequently decreased fluoride concentrations, many of which (~40%) contained repetitive extragenic palindromic (REP) sequences. Northern blot analysis of some of these highly upregulated REP-containing transcripts (i.e., <i>osmC</i>, <i>proP</i>, <i>efeO</i> and <i>yghA</i>) confirmed their considerably enhanced abundance in response to NaF treatment. An mRNA stability analysis of <i>osmC</i> and <i>yghA</i> transcripts demonstrated that fluoride treatment slows down RNA degradation, thereby enhancing RNA stability and steady-state mRNA levels. Moreover, we demonstrate that turnover of these transcripts depends on RNase E activity and RNA degradosome. Thus, we show that NaF exerts significant effects at the whole-transcriptome level under hypoxic growth (i.e., mimicking the host environment), and fluoride can impact gene expression posttranscriptionally by slowing down ATP-dependent degradation of structured RNAs. <b>IMPORTANCE</b> Gram-negative <i>Escherichia coli</i> is a rod-shaped facultative anaerobic bacterium commonly found in microaerobic/anaerobic environments, including the dental plaques of warm-blooded organisms. These latter can be treated efficiently with fluoride-rich compounds that act as anticaries agents to prevent tooth decay. Although fluoride inhibits microbial growth by affecting metabolic pathways, the molecular mechanisms underlying its activity under anaerobic conditions remain poorly defined. Here, using genome-wide transcriptomics, we explore the impact of fluoride treatments on <i>E. coli</i> gene expression under anaerobic conditions. We reveal key gene clusters associated with cellular responses to fluoride and define its ATP-dependent stabilizing effects on transcripts containing repetitive extragenic palindromic sequences. We demonstrate the mechanisms controlling the RNA stability of these REP-containing mRNAs. Thus, fluoride can affect gene expression posttranscriptionally by stabilizing structured RNAs.","affiliation":"Institute of Molecular Biology, Academia Sinica, Taipei, Taiwan.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Ministry of Science and Technology, Taiwan (MOST)","grantId":"MOST 107-2311-B-001-029-MY3","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Academia Sinica","grantId":"AS-IA-110-LO3","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Academia Sinica","grantId":"AS2323","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Ministry of Science and Technology, Taiwan","grantId":"MOST 107-2311-B-001-029-MY3","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Academia Sinica (AS)","grantId":"AS-IA-110-LO3","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Academia Sinica (AS)","grantId":"AS2323","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Sodium fluoride","Gene regulation","Posttranscriptional Regulation","Mrna Degradation","Anaerobic Growth"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1128/spectrum.04158-22"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC10100675"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC10100675?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":13,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedT