,"pubYear":"2020","pageInfo":"21671-21676","abstractText":"Paramagnetic chemical exchange saturation transfer (paraCEST) agents are well-suited for imaging tissue pH because the basis of CEST, chemical exchange, is inherently sensitive to pH. Several previous pH-sensitive paraCEST agents were based on an exchanging Ln<sup>3+</sup> -bound water molecule as the CEST antenna but this design often added additional line-broadening to the bulk water signal due to T<sub>2</sub> exchange. We report herein a pH-sensitive paraCEST agent that lacks an inner-sphere water molecule but contains one Ln-bound -OH group for CEST activation. The Yb<sup>3+</sup> complex, Yb(1), displayed a single, highly shifted CEST peak originating from the exchangeable Yb-OH proton, the frequency of which changed over the biologically relevant pH range. CEST images of phantoms ranging in pH from 6 to 8 demonstrate the potential of this agent for imaging pH. Initial rodent imaging studies showed that Gd(1) remains in the vascular system much longer than anticipated but is cleared slowly via renal filtration.","affiliation":"Advanced Imaging Research Center, University of Texas Southwestern Medical Center, Dallas, TX, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIBIB NIH HHS","acronym":"EB","grantId":"EB-02584","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","grantId":"S10 OD023552","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institute of Biomedical Imaging and Bioengineering","grantId":"EB-02584","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NHLBI NIH HHS","acronym":"HL","grantId":"R01 HL133506","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIBIB NIH HHS","acronym":"EB","grantId":"P41 EB015908","orderIn":0},{"agency":"U.S. Department of Health and Human Services","grantId":"CA-115531","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId