":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1128/JB.187.24.8350-8360.2005"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC1316998"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC1316998?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1128/jb.187.24.8350-8360.2005"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":122,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"Y","dbCrossReferenceList":{"dbName":["ARXPR","EMBL","INTERPRO"]},"hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2006-01-06","dateOfCreation":"2005-12-03","dateOfRevision":"2018-11-13","firstPublicationDate":"2005-12-01","firstIndexDate":"2009-12-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-12","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"18199838","source":"MED","pmid":"18199838","pmcid":"PMC2242690","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC2242690"]},"doi":"10.1073/pnas.0709671105","title":"High-confidence prediction of global interactomes based on genome-wide coevolutionary networks.","authorString":"Juan D, Pazos F, Valencia A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Juan D","lastName":"Juan","firstName":"David","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1912-9667"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Structural Bioinformatics Group, Spanish National Cancer Research Centre, Melchor Fernández Almagro 3, 28029 Madrid, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Pazos F","lastName":"Pazos","firstName":"Florencio","initials":"F","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2646-6226"}},{"fullName":"Valencia A","lastName":"Valencia","firstName":"Alfonso","initials":"A"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2646-6226"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1912-9667"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"105","journalIssueId":1502374,"dateOfPublication":"2008 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2008,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A","title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","essn":"1091-6490","issn":"0027-8424","isoabbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A","nlmid":"7505876"},"printPublicationDate":"2008-01-01"},"pubYear":"2008","pageInfo":"934-939","abstractText":"Interacting or functionally related protein families tend to have similar phylogenetic trees. Based on this observation, techniques have been developed to predict interaction partners. The observed degree of similarity between the phylogenetic trees of two proteins is the result of many different factors besides the actual interaction or functional relationship between them. Such factors influence the performance of interaction predictions. One aspect that can influence this similarity is related to the fact that a given protein interacts with many others, and hence it must adapt to all of them. Accordingly, the interaction or coadaptation signal within its tree is a composite of the influence of all of the interactors. Here, we introduce a new estimator of coevolution to overcome this and other problems. Instead of relying on the individual value of tree similarity between two proteins, we use the whole network of similarities between all of the pairs of proteins within a genome to reassess the similarity of that pair, thereby taking into account its coevolutionary context. We show that this approach offers a substantial improvement in interaction prediction performance, providing a degree of accuracy/coverage comparable with, or in some cases better than, that of experimental techniques. Moreover, important information on the structure, function, and evolution of macromolecular complexes can be inferred with this methodology.","affiliation":"Structural Bioinformatics Group, Spanish National Cancer Research Centre, Melchor Fernández Almagro 3, 28029 Madrid, Spain.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Sensitivity and Specificity"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Genome","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Biological Evolution"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Proteins","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/pmc2242690?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free after 6 months","availabilityCode":"F6","documentStyle":"abs","site":"www.pnas.org","url":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/content/abstract/105/3/934"},{"availability":"Free after 6 months","availabilityCode":"F6","documentStyle":"html","site":"www.pnas.org","url":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/content/full/105/3/934"},{"availability":"Free after 6 months","availabilityCode":"F6","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"www.pnas.org","url":"http://www.pnas.org/cgi/reprint/105/3/934.pdf"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0709671105"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC2242690"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC2242690?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0709671105"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":118,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2008-02-12","dateOfCreation":"2008-01-18","dateOfRevision":"2018-11-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2008-01-16","firstPublicationDate":"2008-01-16","firstIndexDate":"2009-12-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-02","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"17134170","source":"MED","pmid":"17134170","pmcid":"PMC2596995","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC2596995"]},"doi":"10.1021/jp0632236","title":"DFT/TDDFT study of lanthanide(III) mono- and bisporphyrin complexes.","authorString":"Liao MS, Watts JD, Huang MJ.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Liao MS","lastName":"Liao","firstName":"Meng-Sheng","initials":"MS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Chemistry, P. O. Box 17910, Jackson State University, Jackson, Mississippi 39217, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Watts JD","lastName":"Watts","firstName":"John D","initials":"JD"},{"fullName":"Huang MJ","lastName":"Huang","firstName":"Ming-Ju","initials":"MJ"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"48","volume":"110","journalIssueId":1364011,"dateOfPublication":"2006 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2006,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Phys Chem A","title":"The journal of physical chemistry. A","essn":"1520-5215","issn":"1089-5639","isoabbreviation":"J Phys Chem A","nlmid":"9890903"},"printPublicationDate":"2006-12-01"},"pubYear":"2006","pageInfo":"13089-13098","abstractText":"The electronic structure, molecular structure, and electronic spectra of lanthanide(III) mono- and bisporphyrin complexes are investigated using a DFT/TDDFT method. These complexes include YbP(acac), YbP(2), [YbP(2)](+), YbHP(2), and [YbP(2)](-) (where P = porphine and acac = acetylacetonate). To shed some light on the origin of the out-of-plane displacement of Yb in YbP(acac), unligated model systems, namely, planar D(4h) and distorted C(4nu) YbP, were calculated. For comparison, the calculations were also extended to include the C and [Ce(IV)P(2) ](+) systems. Even without an axial ligand, the lanthanide atom lies considerably above the porphyrin plane; the distortion of the YbP molecular structure from a planar D(4h) to the nonplanar C(4nu) symmetry leads to a considerable energy lowering. The axial ligand makes the metal out-of-plane displacement even larger, and it also changes the redox properties of the lanthanide monoporphyrin. The ground-state configurations of YbP(2) and YbHP(2) were determined by considering several possible low-lying states. YbP(2) is confirmed to be a single-hole radical. The special redox properties of the bisporphyrin complexes can well be accounted for by the calculated ionization potentials and electron affinities. The TDDFT results provide a clear description of the UV-vis and near-IR absorption spectra of the various lanthanide porphyrins.","affiliation":"Department of Chemistry, P. O. Box 17910, Jackson State University, Jackson, Mississippi 39217, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Research Support, U.S. Gov't, Non-P.H.S.","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"S06 GM08047","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"S06 GM008047","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descript